WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
Tesla's finalized ai5 processor revives the stalled dojo3 supercomputer, while clearing volume production at TSMC and Samsung. This is Tesla Shorts Time, episode 589. Looking back over the past week, global energy storage shipments surged 83% to 303.4 gigawatt hours in the first half, pointing to robust growth for Tesla's megapack operations. Nevada's Tesla semi-factory will test

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:37.140
50,000 unit annual output next month, showing whether long haul electric trucks can move beyond early fleets.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:47.060
VIN tracking puts Tesla cybercab production at roughly 2,250 units, underscoring the deliberate pace ahead of the Austin rollout.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:53.980
Tesla's FSD active users jumped 56% to 1.48 million in the second quarter.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:10.200
Tesla has finalized the development of its artificial intelligence 5 automotive processor, clearing the way for volume production at both TSMC and Samsung facilities, and simultaneously reviving the previously halted dojo3 supercomputer project.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:19.400
Chief Executive Elon Musk has positioned the artificial intelligence 5 chip, as offering performance on par with Nvidia's hopper architecture.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:26.280
Tesla states that two artificial intelligence 5 units will equal the power of a single blackwall processor.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:36.440
The renewed focus on dojo3 is tightly linked to this silicon roadmap, aligning Tesla's in-vehicle compute efforts with its custom training infrastructure,

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:42.560
with Intel joining as a key packaging partner, using its operations to address capacity constraints.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:54.880
Production partners now include TSMC for front-end fabrication, Samsung for additional capacity, and Intel specifically for advanced packaging steps that resolve earlier bottlenecks.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:04.420
The chip roadmap directly supports both unsupervised FSD inference in vehicles and large-scale training runs for optimists and autonomy models.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:14.740
Battery chemistry decisions shape the cost, range, and longevity trade-offs that ultimately determine whether electric vehicles reach mass adoption outside early buyer segments.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:32.040
Early lithium-ion packs prioritized energy density with nickel-manganese cobalt formulations, yet rising raw material prices and safety concerns pushed manufacturers toward lithium-iron phosphate cells that sacrifice some range for lower cost and longer cycle life.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:46.480
Tesla's 4,680 format adds a structural pack approach that integrates cells directly into the vehicle chassis, reducing part-count and assembly steps while targeting higher energy density than previous cylindrical designs.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:57.180
International approaches diverge on these priorities, with Chinese producers scaling LFP aggressively for domestic and export markets, where price sensitivity is high.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:07.600
European and North American programs have retained higher nickel chemistries for flagship models while exploring sodium-ion and other alternatives for entry-level vehicles.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:21.880
For Tesla, the choice carries direct margin implications, as moving more volume onto 4,680 cells with structural integration could lower pack costs per kilowatt hour and simplify gigafactory workflows.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:29.460
Yield improvements at gigatexas and Nevada remain the visible leading indicators for whether the cost curve bends as planned.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:38.880
Tesla posted that a major update is less than a week away. The announcement came directly from the company's official account without additional details on content.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:45.020
